WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.618
Inference is the process of computing probabilities from the network.

00:00:03.618 --> 00:00:07.135
A query variable is the variable you are asking about.

00:00:07.135 --> 00:00:11.004
An evidence variable is a variable whose state has been observed.

00:00:11.004 --> 00:00:16.281
Hidden or unobserved variables are variables in the model whose states are not currently known.

00:00:16.281 --> 00:00:19.447
The result of inference is often a posterior distribution,

00:00:19.447 --> 00:00:22.612
which describes updated probabilities after the evidence is considered.

00:00:22.612 --> 00:00:24.020
Imagine an equipment model.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.482
If Alarm Active is observed as true,

00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:29.296
the estimated probability of Equipment Fault may increase.

00:00:29.296 --> 00:00:31.758
If Power Available is observed as normal,

00:00:31.758 --> 00:00:34.572
the probability of a power-related cause may decrease.

00:00:34.572 --> 00:00:38.793
The update can move through related variables because the network structure connects

00:00:38.793 --> 00:00:40.552
them through local probability distributions.

00:00:40.552 --> 00:00:45.477
This is evidence propagation: observations in one part of the model change beliefs elsewhere.

00:00:45.477 --> 00:00:48.994
Exact inference methods calculate mathematically precise answers for the model.

00:00:48.994 --> 00:00:53.215
They can be appropriate for small networks or networks with favorable structure.

00:00:53.215 --> 00:00:57.436
But exact inference can become expensive when networks are large, densely connected,

00:00:57.436 --> 00:00:59.195
or have many variable states.

00:00:59.195 --> 00:01:01.306
Approximate inference methods, including sampling approaches,

00:01:01.306 --> 00:01:03.065
trade some precision for scalability.

00:01:03.065 --> 00:01:06.582
They estimate probabilities rather than computing an exact answer directly.

00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:11.155
The important engineering point is that computation quality and model quality are different.

00:01:11.155 --> 00:01:15.376
A precise calculation from a poor graph, poor CPT, biased data source,

00:01:15.376 --> 00:01:17.838
or missing variable can still be misleading.

00:01:17.838 --> 00:01:21.708
Inference should be paired with model validation, sensitivity analysis, and review.

00:01:21.708 --> 00:01:25.929
When evidence arrives, a Bayesian network updates beliefs according to its assumptions.

00:01:25.929 --> 00:01:30.502
The analyst still has to ask whether those assumptions fit the real situation.

00:01:30.502 --> 00:01:32.964
It also matters how evidence is handled.

00:01:32.964 --> 00:01:36.130
Some evidence is direct, such as a sensor reading.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:40.351
Some evidence is indirect, such as a human report, a derived alert,

00:01:40.351 --> 00:01:41.758
or a summary score.

00:01:41.758 --> 00:01:45.979
Evidence may be missing, delayed, noisy, or measured by an unreliable process.

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:51.607
A well-documented model explains what each evidence variable represents and how uncertainty in observation is handled.

00:01:51.607 --> 00:01:56.180
Otherwise, the model may treat weak evidence as stronger than it really is.
